    pharm: "1. AC activated charcoal: Only if <4 hours post-ingestion and intact airway. NOT indicated here (8 hours post). 2. ANTIDOTE: IV Acetylcysteine (NAC) — start IMMEDIATELY. Three-bag protocol: Loading: 150 mg/kg in 5% dextrose 200mL over 1 hour. Second: 50 mg/kg in 500mL over 4 hours. Third: 100 mg/kg in 1000mL over 16 hours. Total: 300 mg/kg over 21 hours. 3. Continue NAC beyond 21 hours if: INR >2, ALT >1000, encephalopathy, or paracetamol level still detectable. NAC is most effective if started within 8 hours but still beneficial up to 24+ hours. 4. IV fluids: Maintain hydration. 5. Monitor for NAC adverse effects: anaphylactoid reaction (flushing, rash, bronchospasm, hypotension) — usually during loading dose. If occurs: temporarily stop NAC, give antihistamines (Chlorpheniramine), restart at slower rate. 6. Do NOT give activated charcoal after NAC started (may adsorb NAC). 7. N-acetylcysteine can be given PO (140 mg/kg loading, then 70 mg/kg q4h x 17 doses) if IV NAC unavailable — but IV is preferred.",
