    pmh: "No significant PMH. No known allergies. No regular medications. Young, previously healthy.. Also has Hypothyroidism.",
    medHx: "No regular medications.",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Excess secretions (salivation, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea). Profuse diaphoresis. Constricted pupils (pinpoint, 1mm bilaterally). Muscle fasciculations (face, shoulders, limbs). Generalised weakness. HR 54 bpm (bradycardia), BP 100/60, RR 28 (tachypnoea), SpO2 89% on room air. Auscultation: bilateral crackles and wheeze (pulmonary oedema due to bronchorrhoea). Altered mental status: Glasgow Coma Scale 12 (confused, drowsy). Foul (garlic-like) odour on breath.",
    vitals: "HR: 147 bpm, BP: 80/44 mmHg, RR: 29 breaths/min, Temp: 40°C, SpO2: 83%",
    labs: "No immediate labs needed to start treatment. Send later: plasma and RBC cholinesterase levels (but treatment should NOT be delayed waiting for results). Baseline: creatinine, K+, glucose, arterial blood gas (to assess respiratory status).",
    imaging: "Chest X-ray: may show pulmonary oedema (aspiration or bronchorrhoea).",
    diagnosis: "Acute Organophosphate Poisoning (Severe) — Cholinergic Crisis due to Chlorpyrifos (dermal + inhalational exposure)",
    ddx: ["Organophosphate Poisoning", "Carbamate Poisoning (no fasciculations, shorter duration)", "Nerve Agent Exposure", "Severe Pneumonia", "Acute Pulmonary Oedema (cardiogenic)"],
    goals: "Atropinisation (counter muscarinic effects), pralidoxime (reactivate cholinesterase), respiratory support, decontamination",
    pharm: "1. DECONTAMINATION: Remove all clothing. Wash skin thoroughly with soap and water (wear gloves — providers must protect themselves). Irrigate eyes if exposed. 2. AIRWAY + BREATHING: Ensure airway patency. Suction secretions. Give O2. Prepare for intubation if GCS <8, unable to protect airway, respiratory failure, or refractory hypoxia. 3. ANTIDOTE 1 — ATROPINE (blocks muscarinic receptors): Give IV Atropine 2mg bolus. Repeat 2mg IV every 5 minutes until atropinisation achieved (drying of secretions, HR >60, pupils dilating). Atropine: no upper limit — may need 20-50mg initially in severe cases. Once atropinised: start infusion (10-20% of loading dose per hour). 4. ANTIDOTE 2 — PRALIDOXIME (reactivates cholinesterase): Give IV Pralidoxime (2-PAM) 1-2g over 15-30 minutes, then 0.5 g/hour IV infusion. OR 1g IM q4-6h. Most effective if given within 12 hours. 5. Seizure control: IV Diazepam 10mg or Midazolam 5-10mg. 6. Atropine test: Give atropine 1mg IV — if no mydriasis or tachycardia, confirms OP poisoning.",
    nonPharm: "Healthcare workers must wear PPE (gloves, gown, mask, eye protection) during decontamination to avoid secondary contamination. Admit to ICU/HDU.",
    carePlan: "EMERGENCY: Decontaminate immediately. Secure airway (intubate if needed). Start atropine (2mg IV q5min until atropinised). Give pralidoxime 2g IV loading. Admit to ICU. IV fluids. Continuous monitoring. Psychiatric evaluation (intentional poisoning — suicide attempt may be involved).",
    dtps: "1. Life-threatening cholinergic crisis requiring aggressive atropinisation and possibly intubation. 2. Accidental occupational exposure — highlight need for PPE and safety measures.",
    monitoring: "Continuous: HR, BP, RR, O2 sat, GCS, secretions (atropinisation status). Atropine infusion rate adjusted to keep secretions dry. Labs: serial cholinesterase levels (if available), ABG, electrolytes. Monitor for intermediate syndrome (days 1-4): proximal muscle weakness, respiratory insufficiency. Monitor for delayed neuropathy (2-3 weeks): peripheral neuropathy (organophosphate-induced delayed neuropathy, OIDN).",
    counselling: "You have been poisoned by a pesticide. The treatment involves medications that block the effects of the poison and help clean it from your system. You will be closely monitored in the intensive care unit. This is a serious condition but treatable if we act quickly. We need to discuss how this happened — was it accidental or intentional?",
    followUp: "Monitor atropine requirements (taper as secretions decrease). Continue pralidoxime for 24-48h. Extubate when respiratory function adequate. Psychiatric evaluation if intentional ingestion. Follow-up: neurological exam in 2-3 weeks for delayed neuropathy. Return to work: not before full recovery and with appropriate PPE training.",
