    goals: "Neutralise venom with antivenom, restore coagulation, prevent bleeding complications, manage local effects, monitor for compartment syndrome and secondary infection",
    pharm: "1. ANTIVENOM (IMMEDIATE — do not delay): Polyvalent anti-snake venom (SAIMR or EchITAbG polyvalent) — dose based on severity. For moderate envenomation with coagulopathy: give 2-4 vials IV in 100mL 0.9% NS over 30 minutes. For severe envenomation (VICC + active bleeding): 4-6 vials initially, may repeat in 1-2 hours if coagulopathy persists. Antivenom is the ONLY specific treatment. 2. BLOOD PRODUCTS (if active severe bleeding): Packed Red Blood Cells (transfuse if Hb <7 or active haemorrhage). Cryoprecipitate (fibrinogen <0.5 g/L) — give 10 units. Fresh Frozen Plasma (if coagulopathy persists despite antivenom) — 15mL/kg. Platelet transfusion (if <50,000 and bleeding). 3. WOUND CARE: Clean the bite site with soap and water. Do NOT incise, excise, or apply a tourniquet. Elevation of the limb. Immobilise with a splint. 4. Tetanus prophylaxis: Tetanus toxoid booster if not fully immunised. 5. Antibiotics (only if secondary infection develops): Amoxicillin-clavulanate or Cephalexin. 6. Pain management: Paracetamol 1g QID. Avoid NSAIDs (worsen coagulopathy). Low-dose morphine 0.1mg/kg SC if severe pain refractory to paracetamol. 7. Pressure immobilisation bandage (PIB): Apply a firm crepe bandage from the bite site proximally (similar to a sprained ankle bandage) to slow lymphatic absorption of venom. Do NOT apply a tourniquet.",
    nonPharm: "Pressure immobilisation bandage in situ. Limb elevated. Splint to prevent movement. Avoid any alcohol or aspirin. Ensure the patient does not manipulate the wound.",
    carePlan: "ADMINISTER POLYVALENT ANTIVENOM immediately (2-4 vials IV). Monitor for anaphylaxis to antivenom (give adrenaline 0.5mg IM if severe reaction). Check coagulation profile (20WTCT, PT, fibrinogen) 1-2 hours post-antivenom and repeat q6h. If coagulopathy persists: repeat antivenom dose. Transfuse cryoprecipitate if fibrinogen <0.5. Monitor for compartment syndrome (rising compartment pressures, pain on passive stretch, tense limb). Admit for minimum 24-48 hours observation.",
    dtps: "1. Life-threatening envenomation from a saw-scaled viper (Echis carinatus) — the most common cause of snakebite morbidity and mortality in East Africa. 2. Consumptive coagulopathy requires urgent antivenom and blood products. 3. Compartment syndrome risk requires close monitoring.",
    monitoring: "Coagulation (20WTCT, PT, fibrinogen, platelets) every 2-6 hours until normalised and stable for 24 hours. Limb circumference measured every 2 hours (rising = compartment syndrome). Neurological observation every hour (to rule out delayed neurotoxicity — though uncommon with vipers). Renal function (creatinine, urine output) — venom nephrotoxicity possible. Watch for secondary bleeding (can occur 2-5 days post-bite even after initial treatment).",
    counselling: "You were bitten by a saw-scaled viper, which is one of the most dangerous snakes in Kenya. The venom affects your blood clotting system, which is why you are bleeding from the bite site and gums. We have given you antivenom which will neutralise the venom, and blood products to help your blood clot again. You need to stay in hospital for at least 2 days. The wound may become swollen and painful — this is expected. If you see any new swelling, bleeding, or difficulty breathing, tell a nurse immediately. Do not attempt traditional remedies — they do not work and can cause harm.",
    followUp: "Nephrology/toxicology follow-up in 1 week. Wound review in 3-5 days (watch for secondary infection, tissue necrosis). Coagulation profile at 1 week (may take weeks to normalise fully). Counselling: avoid walking in shambas at night without torch and protective footwear. Wear boots when farming. Most snakes flee from humans — the bite is usually defensive.",
    pearls: "Saw-scaled vipers (Echis carinatus) are the most medically important snakes in East Africa, causing more envenomations and deaths than any other species in Kenya. They are small, nocturnal, and found in rocky areas and agricultural land. The characteristic \"saw-scaling\" behaviour (rubbing body coils together to produce a sizzling sound) is distinctive. The venom is primarily haemotoxic — causing venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy (VICC): destruction of clotting factors, fibrinogen depletion, and thrombocytopenia. The 20WTCT is a simple bedside test: place 2mL of whole blood in a clean glass tube at room temperature — if it does not clot within 20 minutes, VICC is confirmed. Antivenom (polyvalent) is the specific antidote and must be given IV (not IM). Polyvalent antivenom covers viper, cobra, and mamba venoms. In Kenya, the most common venomous snakes are: carpet viper (saw-scaled viper), puff adder, black mamba, and Egyptian cobra. Traditional healers often delay definitive treatment — patient education is critical.",
