    hpi: "Edward Mibei, a 75-year-old Chef from Busia, presents to casualty 24 hours after his last alcoholic drink. Reports drinking approximately 1 bottle of Safari Cane (local spirit, ~40% ABV, ~750mL) daily for the past 8 years. Yesterday, he was admitted to hospital for an unrelated surgical procedure and was unable to drink. Since this morning, he has developed tremors in both hands, profuse sweating, nausea, vomiting, and anxiety. Reports seeing small animals crawling on the walls (visual hallucinations). Denies auditory or tactile hallucinations. No seizures so far. No headache, fever, or neck stiffness. Alert but disoriented to time (knows his name and place but not the date).",
    pmh: "Chronic alcohol use disorder (8 years, heavy daily use). No prior documented alcohol withdrawal or seizures. No liver disease diagnosed. No prior psychiatric admissions.. Also has Hypertension.",
    medHx: "No regular medications. Denies use of other recreational drugs. Does not smoke.",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Tremor: bilateral upper limb resting tremor (coarse). Diaphoresis (profuse sweating). Tachycardia: HR 110 bpm. BP 155/95 (elevated — withdrawal-related sympathetic overactivity). Temp 37.5°C (low-grade, likely from withdrawal). Pupils: dilated bilaterally. Tremor present in hands and tongue. Orientation: person and place intact, time confused (knows month but not exact date). GCS 14 (confused). No nystagmus. No hepatomegaly clinically. No jaundice. No ascites.",
    vitals: "HR: 115 bpm, BP: 154/84 mmHg, RR: 18 breaths/min, Temp: 37.6°C, SpO2: 93%",
    labs: "CIWA-Ar Score: 22 (Moderate withdrawal — score >20 indicates risk for seizures and delirium tremens). Glucose 70 mg/dL (low — common in alcohol withdrawal). WBC 12.5 (mild leukocytosis). Mg 1.2 mg/dL (low, normal 1.7-2.2). AST 85 U/L, ALT 45 U/L (AST:ALT >2 suggests alcoholic liver disease). Bilirubin 1.5 mg/dL (mildly elevated). Albumin 3.2 g/dL. INR 1.3. K+ 3.5 mEq/L. FBC: Hb 13.0 g/dL. Platelets 180,000. Blood alcohol level: 0 mg/dL (currently sober). Urine: negative for other substances.",
    imaging: "No imaging required. If seizures occur: CT head (to exclude structural cause). If concern for Wernicke's: MRI brain (shows mammillary body changes) — but treat empirically without waiting for imaging.",
    diagnosis: "Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome (Moderate-Severe) with Visual Hallucinations — CIWA-Ar 22, at high risk for seizures and delirium tremens",
    ddx: ["Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome", "Alcoholic Hallucinosis (isolated hallucinations without other withdrawal features)", "Delirium Tremens (full syndrome — confusion, autonomic instability, hallucinations, seizures)", "Wernicke's Encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia)", "Hepatic Encephalopathy", "Intracranial Haemorrhage (if seizures occur)"],
    goals: "Prevent seizures and delirium tremens, control autonomic hyperactivity, correct electrolyte abnormalities, prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy, provide nutritional support",
    pharm: "1. BENZODIAZEPINES (first-line for withdrawal): Chlordiazepoxide 20-50mg PO q6h (or Diazepam 10-20mg PO q6h) per CIWA protocol. For CIWA-Ar >20: give Diazepam 20mg PO/IV stat, then 10mg q5min until calm (max 40mg in first hour). Once calm: start regular dosing. 2. Thiamine (Vitamin B1) — CRITICAL to prevent Wernicke's Encephalopathy: IV Thiamine 500mg in 100mL NS over 30 minutes (MUST be given BEFORE glucose/IV fluids). Then 250mg IV TID for 3 days. Then 100mg PO daily long-term. 3. Folic Acid 5mg daily (alcoholics are folate deficient — prevents megaloblastic anaemia). 4. Multivitamin daily (B-complex, especially B6, B12). 5. IV Fluids: 0.9% NS with 40mEq KCl (correct dehydration, hypokalaemia). Add 5% Dextrose when glucose normal and thiamine given. 6. Correct Mg2+: IV Magnesium Sulphate 2g over 15 minutes (Mg deficiency worsens withdrawal and causes seizures). 7. If seizures occur: Diazepam 10mg IV (or Lorazepam 4mg IV). If refractory: Phenobarbital 10-20mg/kg IV. 8. Monitor for delirium tremens: peak 48-72 hours after last drink — if occurs, escalate benzodiazepines and consider ICU admission.",
    nonPharm: "Continuous observation (1:1 nursing if CIWA >20). Seizure precautions (padded side rails, suction, oxygen at bedside). Strict I&O. No physical restraint (can worsen agitation). Calm, well-lit environment. Nutritional support: high-calorie diet once tolerating oral.",
    carePlan: "CIWA-Ar protocol: reassess every 2 hours (or 1 hour if score rising). Give Diazepam 5-10mg q1-2h as needed to keep CIWA <15. Give IV Thiamine 500mg NOW (before any dextrose-containing fluids). Correct Mg2+ and K+. Start regular oral nutrition. If CIWA-Ar >35 or seizures: ICU admission. If visual hallucinations persist despite adequate sedation: consider alcoholic hallucinosis (requires different management — antipsychotics).",
    dtps: "1. Moderate-severe alcohol withdrawal at risk for seizures and delirium tremens. 2. Visual hallucinations present — requires aggressive benzodiazepine management. 3. Nutritional deficiencies (thiamine, folate, magnesium) requiring immediate correction.",
    monitoring: "CIWA-Ar score every 1-2 hours (titrate benzodiazepines to score). Vital signs hourly. Blood glucose every 4-6 hours. Electrolytes (K+, Mg2+, phosphate) every 12-24 hours. Watch for: seizures (peak 12-48 hours), delirium tremens (peak 48-72 hours — 5% mortality if untreated), Wernicke's encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia — requires immediate thiamine). Monitor hepatic function.",
    counselling: "You are experiencing alcohol withdrawal, which can be dangerous. The medications we give will help prevent seizures and reduce the tremors and anxiety. You MUST receive thiamine (vitamin B1) before any sugar-containing fluids — this prevents a serious brain condition called Wernicke's encephalopathy. You will feel better in a few days, but the first 3-5 days are the most critical. After recovery, we need to discuss long-term management of your alcohol use and consider addiction services.",
    followUp: "Nephrology/toxicology review in 24-48 hours. If CIWA stabilises <15 on oral medication for 24h: step down observation. Discharge planning: addiction medicine referral, psychiatric assessment, AA (Alcoholics Anonymous) support groups, hepatology follow-up if liver function abnormal. Benzodiazepine taper over 5-7 days once withdrawal resolved. Long-term thiamine supplementation (100mg daily).",
    pearls: "Alcohol withdrawal syndrome occurs due to rebound CNS excitability after chronic alcohol exposure (alcohol potentiates GABA, so chronic use downregulates GABA receptors and upregulates glutamate receptors — withdrawal removes the alcohol \"brake\" causing hyperexcitability). CIWA-Ar (Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment for Alcohol — Revised) guides benzodiazepine dosing: mild (5-15) = oral chlordiazepoxide, moderate (15-30) = oral diazepam, severe (>30) = IV diazepam + ICU. Delirium tremens (DT) is the most severe form: occurs in 3-5% of alcohol withdrawals, peaks 48-72 hours after last drink, and carries 5% mortality if untreated. Benzodiazepines are the treatment of choice — they replace the missing GABAergic tone. Thiamine deficiency (Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome) is common in chronic alcoholics and can be precipitated by giving glucose before thiamine — always give thiamine first. In Kenya, \"Safari Cane\" (local spirits) is a common cause of alcohol-related presentations. Chronic heavy drinking is also a major risk factor for liver disease, pancreatitis, and oesophageal cancer.",
